---
// Las Cruces, NM page. Family by brand (navy/gold/cream palette, Playfair
// Display + Source Serif 4 + DM Mono, same investigation-first voice),
// NOT by chassis. Every other city page (rio-rancho-nm.astro,
// santa-fe-nm.astro, albuquerque-nm.astro, farmington-nm.astro,
// gallup-nm.astro) shares one page shell: full-bleed photo hero,
// statement-band, 280px sticky left-nav, giant ghost section numerals,
// SEO accordion, navy contact-closer. Tim's word for that was "Siamese
// twins" -- same family is supposed to mean siblings (shared genes,
// different bodies), not one shared body with different faces drawn on.
//
// This page deliberately breaks that shell:
//  - No photo hero. A navy hero carrying an actual river-line SVG motif,
//    since the Rio Grande is the organizing idea of the whole page.
//  - No 280px sticky sidebar. A slim fixed dot-rail on the right edge
//    instead, so the content column is full-width and centered.
//  - No giant corner ghost-numerals. Small "River Mile" labels folded
//    into the existing section-label row.
//  - No separate statement-band. Its one line folded directly into the
//    hero.
// Content components that actually serve the information (zone-strip,
// stat-block, warning/SEO accordions, fact-box, pull-quotes) are kept --
// those aren't the thing that was repeating, the shell was.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = 'Foundation Repair in Las Cruces, NM | TLS Foundations';
const description =
  'Foundation repair in Las Cruces, NM. River-valley clay, La Mesa terrace collapsible soil, and differential movement on former agricultural land -- TLS Foundations investigates before recommending.';
const summary =
  "Las Cruces sits on three different grounds, not one -- fine clay and silt near the Rio Grande, collapsible alluvium on the La Mesa terrace, and former agricultural land now carrying decades of uneven moisture and fill history. We've seen differential movement on converted farmland directly, and it's well-documented wherever irrigated land becomes a subdivision, which is exactly why a proper investigation matters more than assuming which ground a given house has.";

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@graph': [
      {
        '@type': 'Article',
        datePublished: '2026-11-19',
        headline: 'Foundation Repair in Las Cruces, NM',
        description,
        author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'TLS Foundations', url: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com' },
        publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'TLS Foundations', url: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com' },
        about: { '@type': 'Place', name: 'Las Cruces, New Mexico' },
        mainEntityOfPage: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/las-cruces-nm',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'LocalBusiness',
        name: 'TLS Foundations',
        telephone: '+15059914180',
        url: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com',
        address: {
          '@type': 'PostalAddress',
          streetAddress: '5048 Edith Blvd NE Suite E',
          addressLocality: 'Albuquerque',
          addressRegion: 'NM',
          postalCode: '87107',
          addressCountry: 'US',
        },
        areaServed: [
          { '@type': 'City', name: 'Las Cruces', addressRegion: 'NM' },
          { '@type': 'City', name: 'Mesilla', addressRegion: 'NM' },
        ],
        description:
          'Foundation repair contractor serving Las Cruces and the Mesilla Valley, NM. Slab repair, foundation inspection, settlement, heave, helical piers, drainage correction. 55 combined years of New Mexico soil experience. Investigation-first, owner-operated.',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'FAQPage',
        mainEntity: [
          {
            '@type': 'Question',
            name: 'What causes foundation problems in Las Cruces, NM?',
            acceptedAnswer: {
              '@type': 'Answer',
              text: "Las Cruces sits on three different grounds, not one. Depending on the specific lot, the cause can be expansive clay and silt near the Rio Grande, collapsible alluvium on the La Mesa terrace, or differential movement on land that used to be irrigated farmland. The mix varies by address, not by a single town-wide soil type.",
            },
          },
          {
            '@type': 'Question',
            name: 'Does land that used to be farmland have different soil?',
            acceptedAnswer: {
              '@type': 'Answer',
              text: "It can. Much of the Mesilla Valley's newer growth sits on former chile, pecan, or onion fields -- flood-irrigated for generations before being leveled and subdivided. Old field boundaries, filled-in irrigation ditches, and decades of uneven moisture conditioning can leave inconsistent bearing ground beneath what looks like one uniform lot, which can show up as differential movement.",
            },
          },
          {
            '@type': 'Question',
            name: 'How much does foundation repair cost in Las Cruces?',
            acceptedAnswer: {
              '@type': 'Answer',
              text: 'Cost follows cause. A drainage correction and a pier system differ by an order of magnitude, and both get recommended for the same symptoms by different companies. Without a soil evaluation behind an estimate, a wide spread between quotes reflects the companies more than the house.',
            },
          },
          {
            '@type': 'Question',
            name: 'Are helical piers or push piers used for foundation repair in Las Cruces?',
            acceptedAnswer: {
              '@type': 'Answer',
              text: "Both get used in this area, and the choice depends on what's actually under a given slab -- the depth and consistency of bearing material, and whether the mechanism is heave, collapsible settlement, or differential movement from former farmland. Piers installed for the wrong mechanism don't fix the problem, and in some configurations make it worse.",
            },
          },
          {
            '@type': 'Question',
            name: 'Who does foundation repair in Las Cruces, NM?',
            acceptedAnswer: {
              '@type': 'Answer',
              text: 'TLS Foundations serves Las Cruces and the Mesilla Valley with 55 combined years of New Mexico soil and foundation repair experience. Owner-operated, affiliated with SandiaGEO for geotechnical investigation when a site warrants it. Call (505) 991-4180 -- you reach the owner(s).',
            },
          },
        ],
      },
      {
        '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
        itemListElement: [
          { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com' },
          { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Southern New Mexico', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/southern-new-mexico' },
          { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Las Cruces, NM', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/las-cruces-nm' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },
];

const sections = [
  { id: 'what-we-see', label: 'What We See' },
  { id: 'history', label: 'History' },
  { id: 'geology', label: 'Geology' },
  { id: 'zones', label: 'Three Grounds' },
  { id: 'approach', label: 'Our Approach' },
  { id: 'difference', label: 'Before You Sign' },
  { id: 'questions', label: 'Common Questions' },
];
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  schema={schema}
  footer="minimal"
  ogImage="/images/southern-new-mexico-hero.webp"
  summary={summary}
  pageName="Las Cruces"
  nextPage={{ path: '/southern-new-mexico', name: 'Southern New Mexico' }}
>
  <div class="lc-page">
    <div class="mobile-progress" id="mobileProgress"></div>

    <div class="hero">
      <svg class="hero-river" viewBox="0 0 1440 160" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M0,60 C220,30 340,90 580,60 C820,30 940,90 1180,60 C1300,45 1380,60 1440,52" />
        <path d="M0,110 C220,85 340,135 580,110 C820,85 940,135 1180,110 C1300,98 1380,110 1440,104" />
        <path class="hero-river-bright" d="M0,10 C220,-15 340,35 580,10 C820,-15 940,35 1180,10 C1300,0 1380,10 1440,4" />
      </svg>
      <div class="hero-content">
        <span class="hero-city">Las Cruces, New Mexico</span>
        <h1 class="hero-h1">
          <span class="w1">Deep</span><span class="w2">roots.</span>
          <span class="w3">Straight answers.</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="hero-sub">Foundation repair in the Mesilla Valley — Las Cruces, Mesilla, and the Rio Grande communities around them. A century and a half of irrigation built this valley's reputation. It also built some of what's sitting under your slab. We investigate what's actually under a specific address before we recommend a repair, not after.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <nav class="river-nav" aria-label="Page sections">
      <div class="river-nav-track">
        {sections.map((s) => (
          <a href={`#${s.id}`} class="river-dot" data-label={s.label} aria-label={s.label}></a>
        ))}
      </div>
      <a class="river-nav-call" href="tel:5059914180" aria-label="Call (505) 991-4180">
        <span class="river-nav-call-dot"></span>
      </a>
    </nav>

    <div class="content-area">
      <section class="section" id="what-we-see">
        <div class="section-inner">
          <span class="section-label-text" style="display:block;margin-bottom:8px;">River Mile 01 · What We See</span>
          <h2 class="section-heading reveal">Foundation repair in Las Cruces — <em>what's actually happening under your home</em></h2>
          <div class="reveal">
            <p class="lede">A foundation problem in Las Cruces is not automatically one thing. The Mesilla Valley is a working river valley — fine sediment near the Rio Grande, a collapsible terrace east of it, and well over a century of irrigated farmland that's been turned into subdivisions one field at a time.</p>
            <p><mark class="key">The ground under a Las Cruces home depends on which of those three histories it sits on — river-valley clay and silt, the La Mesa terrace's collapsible alluvium, or former agricultural land with its own uneven moisture and fill history.</mark> None of them are evenly distributed, and none of them announce themselves from the street.</p>
            <p>The practical result: a repair that worked at a house a few blocks away isn't automatically the right repair for yours. Similar-looking symptoms — a sloping floor, a cracked slab — don't establish the same cause. The repair should follow what's actually found, not the other way around.</p>
            <p>Foundation inspection here means understanding which of the valley's three ground histories a specific address actually sits on, not assuming because of what the neighborhood looks like.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="history">
        <div class="section-inner">
          <div class="section-label"><span class="section-label-bar"></span><span class="section-label-text">River Mile 02 · History</span></div>
          <h2 class="section-heading reveal">Built on irrigation. <em>Still settling into it.</em></h2>
          <div class="two-col reveal">
            <div>
              <p>New Mexico State University traces its roots to Las Cruces College, which opened in a two-room adobe building in 1888. It became the land-grant college the following year and has anchored the city — and its identity as Aggie country — ever since.</p>
              <p>Much of the valley's identity comes from what's been grown in it. NMSU horticulturist Fabián García released New Mexico No. 9 in 1921, a chile variety that helped found the state's commercial chile industry, and he's credited with planting some of the Mesilla Valley's first pecan trees. Onions have long been grown alongside both. All of it depended on the same thing: well over a century of irrigation pulled from the Rio Grande.</p>
              <p><mark class="key">That irrigation history matters for more than the chile.</mark> Decades of flood-irrigated farmland, later leveled and subdivided for housing, didn't leave uniform ground behind — it left old field boundaries, ditch lines, and uneven moisture conditioning that a new subdivision's grading doesn't erase.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="fact-box">
                <span class="section-label-text" style="display:block;margin-bottom:14px;color:var(--gold);">The Valley, By Geography</span>
                <p>Las Cruces sits in the Mesilla Valley, in the Chihuahuan Desert, with the Organ Mountains' steep rock spires rising immediately east of the city and the Rio Grande running through the valley floor to the west. Desert, river, and mountain, all close together — that combination is part of why the ground here isn't one simple story.</p>
                <p>The historic village of Mesilla, with its well-known plaza, sits right in the river corridor southwest of downtown Las Cruces — some of the oldest continuously settled ground in the valley.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pull reveal">Chile and pecans need irrigation to grow. Foundations need to know where that water history actually went.</div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="geology">
        <div class="section-inner">
          <div class="section-label"><span class="section-label-bar"></span><span class="section-label-text">River Mile 03 · Geology</span></div>
          <h2 class="section-heading reveal">Three ground histories, <em>one valley</em></h2>
          <p class="reveal" style="max-width:100%;">The Mesilla Valley's ground is shaped by three different histories, not one — and none of them show up by looking at a house from the street.</p>

          <div class="stat-block reveal">
            <div class="stat-item">
              <span class="stat-num" data-target="1888" data-no-comma="true">1888</span>
              <span class="stat-label">NMSU founded</span>
              <p class="stat-desc">As a two-room adobe building — this valley has been settled and built on for well over a century</p>
            </div>
            <div class="stat-divider"></div>
            <div class="stat-item">
              <span class="stat-num" data-target="1921" data-no-comma="true">1921</span>
              <span class="stat-label">New Mexico No. 9 released</span>
              <p class="stat-desc">Fabián García's chile variety, and one more marker of how long this valley has been under irrigation</p>
            </div>
            <div class="stat-divider"></div>
            <div class="stat-item">
              <span class="stat-num" data-target="55">55<span>+</span></span>
              <span class="stat-label">Combined years<br />in NM soils</span>
              <p class="stat-desc">TLS Foundations and <a href="https://www.sandiageo.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SandiaGEO</a> combined</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pull reveal">None of these numbers tell you what's under a specific address. That's what an investigation is for.</div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="zones">
        <div class="section-inner">
          <div class="section-label"><span class="section-label-bar"></span><span class="section-label-text">River Mile 04 · Three Grounds</span></div>
          <h2 class="section-heading reveal">Foundation repair across <em>three different grounds</em></h2>
          <p class="lede reveal">Las Cruces isn't one soil story. Depending on where a lot sits in the valley, it's river-deposited clay, collapsible terrace alluvium, or former farmland with its own fill history. We don't assume which one from the address — we check.</p>

          <div class="zone-strip reveal">
            <div class="zone-strip-num"><span>01</span></div>
            <div class="zone-strip-content">
              <h3 class="zone-strip-title">River Bottom</h3>
              <div class="zone-strip-tag">Rio Grande Corridor · Fine Sediment · Expansive Clay</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="two-col reveal">
            <div>
              <p>Ground closest to the Rio Grande and historic Mesilla carries fine river and lake sediment — clay and silt deposited over thousands of years of flooding, long before the valley was leveed and irrigated on a schedule. Where that clay is present, it's genuinely expansive: it swells when wet and shrinks when dry, and it can heave a foundation built on it. This is also the valley's oldest settled ground — Old Mesilla itself sits right in this corridor.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="zone-warnings">
                <span class="zone-warning-label">Warning signs — River Bottom homes</span>
                <ul class="warning-list">
                  <li class="warning-item">
                    <button class="warning-trigger"><span class="warning-trigger-text">Cracking that tracks wet and dry seasons</span><span class="warning-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                    <div class="warning-drawer"><div class="warning-drawer-inner">Expansive clay responds to moisture on a seasonal cycle. Damage that seems to track wet and dry years, rather than appearing once and staying put, points toward heave rather than simple settlement.</div></div>
                  </li>
                  <li class="warning-item">
                    <button class="warning-trigger"><span class="warning-trigger-text">Older homes in or near Old Mesilla</span><span class="warning-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                    <div class="warning-drawer"><div class="warning-drawer-inner">The oldest, most river-adjacent ground in the valley. Age alone isn't a verdict, but proximity to the river corridor is a reason to look closely at which mechanism is actually present.</div></div>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="zone-strip reveal">
            <div class="zone-strip-num"><span>02</span></div>
            <div class="zone-strip-content">
              <h3 class="zone-strip-title">La Mesa Terrace</h3>
              <div class="zone-strip-tag">Terrace Alluvium · Collapsible Soil · First-Wetting Settlement</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="two-col reveal">
            <div>
              <p>East of the river, much of Las Cruces sits on the La Mesa terrace — older, dry-deposited alluvium that stayed stable for thousands of years because it stayed dry. Irrigation and urban development moisture have triggered first-wetting collapse in a number of mid-20th-century Las Cruces foundations: the soil holds its shape until water reaches it for the first time, then loses volume rapidly, sometimes decades after a house was built.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="zone-warnings">
                <span class="zone-warning-label">Warning signs — La Mesa Terrace homes</span>
                <ul class="warning-list">
                  <li class="warning-item">
                    <button class="warning-trigger"><span class="warning-trigger-text">Settlement that seems to appear with no clear trigger</span><span class="warning-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                    <div class="warning-drawer"><div class="warning-drawer-inner">Collapsible soil stays stable while dry, then loses volume rapidly the first time sustained moisture reaches it — a first-wetting event, not a slow process. That can happen decades after a house was built, with no obvious cause from the outside.</div></div>
                  </li>
                  <li class="warning-item">
                    <button class="warning-trigger"><span class="warning-trigger-text">Mid-century homes after a landscaping or irrigation change</span><span class="warning-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                    <div class="warning-drawer"><div class="warning-drawer-inner">New landscaping, a new sprinkler system, or a changed drainage pattern can be exactly the kind of sustained moisture that triggers first-wetting collapse in terrace soil that's been dry for decades.</div></div>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="zone-strip reveal">
            <div class="zone-strip-num"><span>03</span></div>
            <div class="zone-strip-content">
              <h3 class="zone-strip-title">Former Farmland</h3>
              <div class="zone-strip-tag">Converted Agricultural Land · Differential Fill · Uneven Moisture History</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="two-col reveal">
            <div>
              <p><mark class="key">Much of the Mesilla Valley's newer growth sits on what used to be chile, pecan, or onion fields — flood-irrigated for generations before it was leveled and subdivided.</mark> That history doesn't disappear under a new slab. Old field boundaries, filled-in irrigation ditches, and decades of uneven moisture conditioning can leave inconsistent bearing ground across what looks like one uniform lot, and that can show up as differential movement — one part of a foundation settling or heaving differently than the rest.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="zone-warnings">
                <span class="zone-warning-label">Warning signs — Former farmland subdivisions</span>
                <ul class="warning-list">
                  <li class="warning-item">
                    <button class="warning-trigger"><span class="warning-trigger-text">Movement that's worse on one side or corner of the house</span><span class="warning-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                    <div class="warning-drawer"><div class="warning-drawer-inner">A classic signature of differential movement — one part of the foundation reacting to different ground than the rest, often tracing back to an old field boundary or ditch line nobody mapped before building.</div></div>
                  </li>
                  <li class="warning-item">
                    <button class="warning-trigger"><span class="warning-trigger-text">Newer construction on land that was farmland within living memory</span><span class="warning-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                    <div class="warning-drawer"><div class="warning-drawer-inner">Doesn't mean there's a problem. It means the fill and moisture history under that slab is worth understanding before assuming the ground is as uniform as the new subdivision looks.</div></div>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pull reveal">Three different grounds, sometimes within the same subdivision. That's exactly why we investigate before we recommend anything.</div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="approach">
        <div class="section-inner">
          <div class="section-label"><span class="section-label-bar"></span><span class="section-label-text">River Mile 05 · Our Approach</span></div>
          <h2 class="section-heading reveal">Foundation investigation in Las Cruces — <em>before everything else</em></h2>
          <div class="two-col reveal">
            <div>
              <p><mark class="key">Here's what a same-day quote can't account for: which of the valley's three grounds is actually under that specific slab, at what depth, and what the water has been doing to it for decades.</mark> A heaving-clay lot and a collapsible-terrace lot a few streets apart are different problems with different answers. Nobody can tell them apart from the sidewalk.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="pull">We'd rather tell you nothing is wrong than sell you something that is.</div>
              <p>TLS Foundations works with <a href="https://www.sandiageo.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SandiaGEO</a>, a New Mexico geotechnical investigation firm, when site conditions warrant a formal soil investigation before repair is recommended. Sometimes the honest answer is drainage correction. Sometimes it's monitoring and nothing else. The investigation is what tells you which.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="difference">
        <div class="section-inner">
          <div class="section-label"><span class="section-label-bar"></span><span class="section-label-text">River Mile 06 · Before You Sign</span></div>
          <h2 class="section-heading reveal">Not a pitch — <em>a few questions worth asking first</em></h2>
          <div class="two-col reveal">
            <div>
              <p>Ask which of the valley's three grounds you're actually on. River bottom, La Mesa terrace, and former farmland don't take the same repair, and a quote written before anyone's checked which one is under your slab is a guess with a price tag attached. TLS works with <a href="https://www.sandiageo.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SandiaGEO</a> when a formal soil investigation is warranted — before a repair method is named, not after.</p>
              <p><mark class="key">Ask if your lot used to be a field.</mark> If a subdivision was chile, pecan, or onion fields within living memory, old field boundaries and irrigation ditches don't disappear when the land gets leveled — they just go underground. A pier plan written without knowing that is a design written on assumption.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="pull">Ask who actually shows up. Not everyone who answers the phone is who ends up standing on your lot.</div>
              <p>55 combined years working New Mexico soils. In Las Cruces, that means the person who looks at your home is the person who answers the phone — not a scheduler, not a commissioned rep passing through on a route. If your foundation is fine, we'll tell you that and leave.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="questions">
        <div class="section-inner">
          <div class="section-label"><span class="section-label-bar"></span><span class="section-label-text">River Mile 07 · Common Questions</span></div>
          <h2 class="section-heading reveal">Foundation repair questions <em>Las Cruces homeowners ask</em></h2>
          <p class="reveal" style="max-width:680px;">Methods, costs, and what to expect — answered for this market specifically, not in general.</p>

          <div class="seo-accordion reveal">
            <div class="seo-item">
              <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Slab repair in Las Cruces, NM</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
              <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Slab-on-grade construction is common in Las Cruces, so slab repair is a frequent question in this market. The right approach depends on the mechanism: a slab settling into collapsible terrace soil needs a different answer than a slab being lifted by expanding river-valley clay, or one moving unevenly over former farmland. Those can look similar from inside the house and require different responses.</div></div>
            </div>
            <div class="seo-item">
              <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Foundation repair cost in Las Cruces</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
              <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Cost follows cause. A drainage correction and a pier system are separated by an order of magnitude, and both get recommended for the same set of symptoms by different companies. If estimates in this area vary wildly with no soil evaluation behind any of them, that spread is telling you about the companies, not the house.</div></div>
            </div>
            <div class="seo-item">
              <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Does land that used to be farmland have different soil?</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
              <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">It can. Much of the Mesilla Valley's newer growth sits on former chile, pecan, or onion fields, flood-irrigated for generations before being leveled and subdivided. Old field boundaries, filled-in irrigation ditches, and decades of uneven moisture conditioning can leave inconsistent bearing ground beneath what looks like one uniform lot — which is exactly why an investigation at the specific address matters more than an assumption based on the subdivision.</div></div>
            </div>
            <div class="seo-item">
              <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Helical or push piers — which one for Las Cruces?</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
              <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Both get used here, and the choice depends on what's actually under a given slab — the depth and consistency of bearing material, and whether the mechanism is heave, collapsible settlement, or differential movement from former farmland. Piers specified without that information are a design written on assumption rather than evidence.</div></div>
            </div>
            <div class="seo-item">
              <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">What's different about homes near Mesilla or the river?</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
              <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Ground closest to the Rio Grande and historic Mesilla carries more fine river and lake sediment — clay that's genuinely expansive, swelling when wet and shrinking when dry. It's not a guarantee of a problem, but it's a reason to check for heave specifically rather than assume settlement.</div></div>
            </div>
            <div class="seo-item">
              <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Do I need a soil investigation, or just a contractor?</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
